karr #38 implemented creation_rules_for: find the config, match the first rule, return recipients plus the encryption rule. What it deliberately did NOT do, each its own decision:

 - destination_rules. Not read at all; a config carrying only destination_rules is reported as having no creation rules.
 - key_groups / shamir_threshold. REFUSED rather than ignored (a rule carrying either dies), because sops writes shamir_threshold into the document it produces and splits the data key across groups. Implementing them means implementing Shamir, which this distribution cannot do.
 - Recipients for backends other than age - pgp, kms, gcp_kms, azure_keyvault, hc_vault_transit_uri. Also refused; blocked on karr #39. NOTE for whoever picks that up: those are the CONFIG file field names and they are NOT the sops section field names (azure_keyvault vs azure_kv, hc_vault_transit_uri vs hc_vault). Measured on 3.13.3: aws_kms, azure_kv and hc_vault in a creation rule are ignored by sops entirely.
 - $SOPS_CONFIG. sops honours it; creation_rules_for does not, on purpose - an environment variable that redirects which public keys a secret gets encrypted to is a reasonable thing for a user to set for a command they typed and not a reasonable thing for a library to obey for a caller who never asked. config => $ENV{SOPS_CONFIG} opts in. Revisit only with a reason.
 - The full --config search rules.

Split out of #38.
Added explicit POD section 'What this does not read or return' documenting destination_rules (not read), aws_kms/azure_kv/hc_vault in a creation rule (ignored, with the section-vs-config name asymmetry called out), and . No code change.
